  "subtitle": "The talking car whose 'voice' was a tiny vinyl record with six parallel grooves",
  "why_scout_cares": "Nissan made its 1981-84 luxury cars speak using the most analog voice possible: a 3-inch white plastic phonograph record cut with six parallel grooves, spun by a rubber belt inside a box under the dash, with a tiny tonearm that drops onto the right groove when a door opens or the fuel runs low. It is a genuine, commercially shipped talking-computer precursor built entirely from record-player parts — a one-of-a-kind interaction mechanism in this museum, and the perfect analog counterpart to every synthesized-voice artifact we hold.",
